{"A":"Develop the primary sequential candidate: stream input into an O(N^2) two-stage rolling sum. Let W=N-S+1; retain only S rows of W horizontal S-cell sums, update W vertical sums per row, and take the maximum after row S-1. Use signed 64-bit accumulators/ring values, initialize maxima from the first valid window, and handle negative values. This cuts memory versus the source’s matrix and avoids its O(S(N-S+1)^2) traversal. Validate S=1, S=N-1, and signed/extreme inputs; do not rely on fixed cases.","B":"Research a separate four-core candidate, not an assumed addition to A. Parse/store a flat matrix, partition output-row ranges among workers, give each worker private vertical workspace and a local maximum, and initialize each stripe correctly with its S source rows. Avoid races, false sharing, and nested/barrier-heavy row parallelism; adapt or decline parallelism when there are too few output rows. Compare its full end-to-end cost, including serial input and extra memory, against the sequential rolling design.","C":"Own low-level throughput work usable by either algorithm: replace mixed iostream/scanf parsing with one robust buffered signed-integer scanner, use contiguous/aligned-friendly storage, and assess fused versus separate horizontal/vertical update loops for ARM64 auto-vectorization/cache behavior. Preserve arbitrary whitespace, signs, exact output, and 64-bit sum safety. Benchmark parser and kernel changes independently; input is an unavoidable O(N^2) cost, so prioritize end-to-end rather than synthetic kernel gains.","G":"Integrate only measured-compatible candidates under the fixed build, target, oracle, and budgets. Baseline is correct on 4 cases and averages 0.1313 s (0.1252–0.1395) on 4-core ARM64, with no workload profile, so require gains beyond noise and do not assume A/B/C gains compose. Compare baseline, A, A+C, B+C, and any justified hybrid; retain the fastest correct whole program. Recheck all-negative matrices, S=1, S=N-1, ties, whitespace/sign parsing, overflow-safe arithmetic, peak memory, and exact single-line output; never hardcode inputs or expected results.","analysis":"Only Problem B source is present. Its vector-of-vectors storage, mixed text input, and S-wide snake updates leave an O(N^2) streaming rolling-window design as the main algorithmic opportunity, while text parsing may dominate at the observed short baseline time. Use 64-bit intermediates because valid SxS arithmetic can exceed int even though the reported maximum fits int32; treat threading as an empirical hypothesis rather than a guaranteed speedup."}